    "result_text": "I've traced the full picture. Here's the situation:\n\n## Result: the requirement is already satisfied, and the suite is green\n\nEvery rejection path in the app funnels through `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, and all of the 400/409/429 handlers share one identical response-building path:\n\n- **400 Bad Request** \u2014 `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`\n- **409 Conflict** \u2014 `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException`, `OwnerCityFullException`\n- **429 Too Many Requests** \u2014 `OwnerDailyLimitException`\n\nEach calls `detailBuild(...)` and returns `ResponseEntity.status(status).body(problemDetail)`. `detailBuild` (line 64) populates a Spring `ProblemDetail` with all four RFC7807 members:\n- `type` (`setType`), `title` (`setTitle`), `status` (via `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`), `detail` (`setDetail`).\n\nReturning a `ProblemDetail` body makes Spring serialize it as `application/problem+json` (Jackson's problem-detail content-type handling), with the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\nI verified this end-to-end rather than by inspection alone:\n\n- The new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (a `POST /api/owners` with `city` removed \u2192 400) asserts `contentTypeCompatibleWith(\"application/problem+json\")` and `$.status == 400`, and it **passes**.\n- Since the 409 and 429 handlers use the byte-for-byte same `detailBuild` \u2192 `ResponseEntity` path that the 400 test exercises, they emit the same content type and member set.\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nNo code change was required \u2014 the `ProblemDetail`-based advice already conforms to the spec, and no other code path produces a 400/409/429 outside it (`BindingErrorsResponse` exists but is unused). If you'd like, I can additionally tailor the `detail` text per outcome (e.g. a rate-limit-specific message for the 429) rather than the shared \"invalid or missing parameters\" string, but that's a wording refinement beyond what the spec's four-member requirement mandates.",
